\description{
Read an mzLib spectral library (.msl) as one row per precursor, or with fragments one row per fragment ion, with mzLib's not-available sentinels as nulls.

Answers \strong{what is in this spectral library?} For each precursor - a peptide or proteoform at
one charge - the m/z, retention time and ion mobility a search expects to see it at, its labels
and provenance, and on request the fragment ions it expects in the MS2 spectrum. MetaMorpheus and
mzLib write libraries in mzLib's binary \code{.msl} format; this reads one with mzLib's own reader,
\code{MslFileTypeHandler.Open} and \code{MslLibrary.GetEntry}.
}
\details{
Rows come in the library's stored order (\code{precursor_index}), not by m/z: sort on \code{precursor_mz}
for that. mzLib's "not available" values - an ion mobility of 0.0, a NaN q-value, an NCE of 0,
an empty protein accession - arrive as \code{NA}.

Large libraries open \strong{index-only}: precursors in memory, fragment blocks left on disk. mzLib
decides by file size (1 GiB by default in the pinned build); pass \code{index_only_threshold} to move
the line. The rows are the same either way.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_library_records}. \code{record_count} counts the precursors in the whole library,
  before \code{offset} and \code{limit}; \code{returned_count} the precursors returned (precursors, not rows: the
  unit \code{limit} and \code{offset} count in), 0 with \code{out}; \code{row_count} the rows of \code{records}, one per
  fragment ion with \code{fragments}; \code{offset} the precursors skipped; \code{truncated} whether precursors
  were left behind.

  The library's facts: \code{reader} (\code{"MslLibrary"}); \code{format_version}, the file's \code{.msl} format
  version; \code{is_compressed} (a compressed library always loads whole) and \code{is_index_only};
  \code{target_count}, precursors that are not decoys, entrapment targets included; \code{decoy_count},
  decoy precursors, entrapment decoys included; \code{proteoform_count}, precursors whose
  \code{molecule_type} is \code{"Proteoform"}; and \code{min_precursor_mz} and \code{max_precursor_mz}, in m/z, \code{NA}
  when the library has no precursors. \code{fragments_included} says whether the rows are fragment
  ions, and \code{index_only_threshold} the threshold applied, in bytes: yours, or mzLib's default.

  \code{file_type} is always \code{NA} (\code{.msl} is not one of mzLib's \code{SupportedFileType} members);
  \code{rows_not_read} is always \code{NA}: rows are not counted for a binary library, because mzLib checks
  the whole file before returning any precursor;
  \code{retention_time_unit} is \code{NA} at the top level, because the unit varies by precursor - it is a
  column. \code{absent_fields} names the columns this library has no source for, so every value is
  \code{NA} (\code{ion_mobility} when no precursor has one, \code{protein_accession} and \code{protein_name} without
  a protein table, \code{gene_name} without gene names); \code{failed_fields} the columns whose read threw on
  some rows; \code{excluded_fields} is always empty; \code{caveats} says what the table cannot be trusted
  to mean. \code{output} says where the table was written, or is \code{NULL}.

  \code{records} is a data.frame, or \code{NULL} with \code{out}. One row per precursor: \code{precursor_index}
  (\strong{1-based in R}, the position in the library and the row order), \code{full_sequence} (mzLib
  bracket notation), \code{base_sequence}, \code{precursor_mz} in m/z, \code{charge_state} (a charge),
  \code{retention_time} in iRT or minutes \strong{per row} and \code{retention_time_unit} (\code{"iRT"} or
  \code{"minutes"}), \code{ion_mobility} in 1/K0 (V s/cm2; \code{NA} where mzLib stores 0.0), \code{is_decoy},
  \code{is_entrapment}, \code{decoy_contaminant_target} (\code{"T"}, \code{"D"}, \code{"ET"} or \code{"ED"}),
  \code{protein_accession}, \code{protein_name} and \code{gene_name} (\code{NA} where mzLib stores an empty string),
  \code{is_proteotypic}, \code{q_value} (a fraction, 0 to 1; \code{NA} where mzLib stores NaN, a predicted
  library), \code{elution_group_id}, \code{molecule_type}, \code{source_type} (\code{"Predicted"}, \code{"Empirical"} or
  \code{"EmpiricalRefined"}), \code{dissociation_type} and \code{nce} (an NCE, a whole number; \code{NA} where mzLib
  stores 0).

  With \code{fragments = TRUE}, one row per fragment ion: those columns, then \code{fragment_count} (fragment
  ions of the precursor), \code{fragment_mz} in m/z, \code{fragment_intensity} (relative intensity within
  the precursor, 0 to 1), \code{product_type}, \code{secondary_product_type} and
  \code{secondary_fragment_number} (\code{NA} for a terminal ion), \code{fragment_number}, \code{residue_position}
  (\code{NA} for a custom-loss fragment, whose stored position mzLib reads back as 0),
  \code{fragment_charge} (a charge), \code{neutral_loss} in Da (0 is no loss), \code{is_internal},
  \code{is_diagnostic}, \code{exclude_from_quant} and \code{annotation}. A precursor with no fragment ions is one
  row with those cells \code{NA}.

}
\section{Three things to know before you use the numbers}{


\strong{Retention time has a unit per row.} A library stores either an indexed retention time (iRT)
or run minutes for each precursor (\code{MslLibraryEntry.RtIsCalibrated}), and one library can hold
both. Read \code{retention_time_unit} on each row; there is no conversion between them without a
calibration against a run, which \code{\link{library_calibrate_rt}} fits.

**"Not available" is \code{NA}, not a number.** A text library converted to \code{.msl} (from \code{.msp}) has
no NCE, dissociation type, q-value, ion mobility or protein, so those are \code{NA} (and
\code{dissociation_type} is \code{"Unknown"}).

\strong{The library is unfiltered.} Decoys (\code{D}, \code{ED}) and entrapment precursors (\code{ET}, \code{ED}) are
rows; filter on \code{is_decoy} or \code{decoy_contaminant_target} before counting targets.

}
